From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: pin the GPU to 1008 MHz on its own PLL

`arm64-dts-a733-enable-the-gpu-at-the-vendor-s-operating-point` pinned CLK_GPU0's parent to PLL_PERI0_600M and gave two reasons. Both
still hold; what has changed is that the rest of the argument has now been
paid for, so the pin can move to the clock it was always meant to end on.

WHY GPUPLL SERVES 1008 MHz WHEN IT COULD NOT SERVE 600

The VCO window is 1.272-2.52 GHz (UM Table 4-2; the driver
enforces it as SUN60IW2_PLL_GPU0_MIN_VCO/MAX_VCO, and the floor is 1272 rather
than 1260 because the VCO is 24 MHz * N). 600 MHz off this PLL would need a
1200 MHz VCO -- below the floor, which is exactly why `arm64-dts-a733-enable-the-gpu-at-the-vendor-s-operating-point` could not use it.
1008 MHz needs 24 MHz * 84 = 2016 MHz, comfortably inside. Of the vendor's
four-row ladder, the top row is the only one this PLL can reach at all.

ONE RATE REQUEST, AND THE MODEL HAS EXACTLY ONE LEGAL ANSWER

gpu0 carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT with CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT, and gpupll
carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, so a 1008 MHz request propagates instead of being
absorbed: gpu0's mask divider takes M = 0 (/1, the only table entry that keeps
the rate), gpupll must then be 1008 MHz, and its P0 must be 2 -- P0 = 1 would
ask the VCO for 1008 MHz and the enforced floor refuses it. VCO 2016, P0 2,
M 0. No value here is chosen; each is the only one the window permits.

The mask divider is why the reachable ladder off this source is 1008 / 504 /
252 / 126 / 63 and nothing between: GPU0_DIV1 masks cycles rather than
counting: GPU0_CLK = Clock Source * ((16-M)/16) (UM 4.1.4.4),
and only M = 0, 8, 12, 14, 15 land on an integer ratio. That the
Armbian tree ships 252/504/1008 is therefore not a source for this
patch -- it is the same three numbers falling out of the same formula.

⚠ `arm64-dts-a733-enable-the-gpu-at-the-vendor-s-operating-point`'s CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT WARNING DOES NOT TRANSFER, and that is precisely
what makes this safe now. It warned that a rate request on gpu0 would propagate
into PLL_PERI0, the PLL that feeds the whole SoC. With GPUPLL as the parent it
propagates into the GPU's own PLL, which drives nothing else: "mem" is that
same PLL and drm/powervr only clk_prepare_enable()s it, never sets a rate on it
(`arm64-dts-a733-gpu-mem-clock-is-the-gpu-pll`, and re-checked in the tree we build).

⚠ THIS PATCH IS NOT SAFE WITHOUT `arm64-dts-a733-dcdc4-is-vdd-gpu-and-it-gets-a-window` IN THE SAME SERIES. The rail boots at
800 mV; 1008 MHz there is an under-volt, and an under-volt does not announce
itself. `arm64-dts-a733-dcdc4-is-vdd-gpu-and-it-gets-a-window` raises DCDC4 to 960 mV at PMIC probe, which is seconds before
powervr probes and this rate is applied.

WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT, on the board, in one boot:

  1. /sys/kernel/debug/clk/gpu0/clk_rate reads 1008000000 and clk_parent reads
     gpupll; pll-gpu0 reads 2016000000.
  2. GPU0_CLK (0x02002b20) reads SEL = 000 and M = 0 with bit 31 set under
     load, i.e. 0x80000000; PLL_GPU0 (0x020020e0) carries PLL_N = 0x53.
  3. DCDC4 reads 960000 uV before the GPU is touched.
  4. the GPU test still ACCEPTs -- same 65536/65536 pixels, at the
     higher clock. A wrong-pixel failure here is the under-volt signature and
     means the rail, not the clock, is what needs revisiting.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -2892,8 +2892,49 @@
 			 * A733 user manual), so 24 MHz x 50 / 2 = 600 needs
 			 * a 1200 MHz VCO, below the floor.
 			 */
+			/*
+			 * 1008 MHz on the GPU's own PLL, which replaces the
+			 * PERI0_600M pin above. The reasoning that put it
+			 * there is unchanged and still true -- it is the
+			 * REST of it that has now been paid for.
+			 *
+			 * Why GPUPLL can serve this rate when it could not
+			 * serve 600: the VCO window is 1.272-2.52 GHz (UM
+			 * Table 4-2, and the driver
+			 * enforces it as MIN_VCO/MAX_VCO). 600 MHz needs a
+			 * 1200 MHz VCO, below the floor. 1008 MHz needs
+			 * 24 MHz x 84 = 2016 MHz, comfortably inside it --
+			 * so of the vendor's ladder, only the top row is
+			 * reachable from this PLL at all.
+			 *
+			 * One rate request, and the model has exactly one
+			 * legal answer. gpu0 carries CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT
+			 * with CLK_SET_RATE_NO_REPARENT, and gpupll carries
+			 * CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT, so 1008 MHz propagates:
+			 * gpu0 mask M=0 (/1) -> gpupll 1008 -> P0 must be 2,
+			 * because P0=1 would ask the VCO for 1008 MHz and
+			 * the enforced floor refuses it. VCO 2016, P0 2,
+			 * M 0. Nothing here is a magic number; every step is
+			 * the only one the window permits.
+			 *
+			 * ⚠ `arm64-dts-a733-enable-the-gpu-at-the-vendor-s-operating-point`'s warning about CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT does
+			 * not transfer, and that is the whole reason this is
+			 * now safe: it warned that a rate on gpu0 would
+			 * propagate into PLL_PERI0, the PLL feeding the whole
+			 * SoC. With GPUPLL as the parent the request
+			 * propagates into the GPU's own PLL, which drives
+			 * nothing else -- "mem" is that same PLL and the
+			 * driver only clk_prepare_enable()s it, never sets a
+			 * rate on it (`arm64-dts-a733-gpu-mem-clock-is-the-gpu-pll`).
+			 *
+			 * ⚠ AND IT IS ONLY SAFE WITH THE DCDC4 NODE IN THE
+			 * SAME SERIES. 1008 MHz at the 800 mV this rail
+			 * boots with is an under-volt, and an under-volt does
+			 * not announce itself.
+			 */
 			assigned-clocks = <&ccu CLK_GPU0>;
-			assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_PLL_PERI0_600M>;
+			assigned-clock-parents = <&ccu CLK_GPUPLL>;
+			assigned-clock-rates = <1008000000>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 	};
